WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.817
This video shows how to move a VMware virtual machine

2
00:00:03.817 --> 00:00:07.297
to OpenShift Virtualization with a warm migration.

3
00:00:07.297 --> 00:00:11.644
A warm migration copies the disks while the virtual machine keeps running.

4
00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:16.141
The source machine shuts down only for the final switch, called the cutover.

5
00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:19.713
We use the network map and the storage map from the last video.

6
00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:24.210
Before we start, here is what the migration connects.

7
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.332
On the left is VMware Cloud Foundation, on IBM Cloud Classic Infrastructure.

8
00:00:29.332 --> 00:00:35.729
Our source virtual machine sits in the lab-4399 folder, and vCenter manages it.

9
00:00:35.729 --> 00:00:41.051
On the right is Red Hat OpenShift Virtualization, on an IBM Cloud VPC.

10
00:00:41.051 --> 00:00:43.898
A transit gateway joins the two sides.

11
00:00:43.898 --> 00:00:47.169
Migration Toolkit for Virtualization, on OpenShift,

12
00:00:47.169 --> 00:00:51.046
connects to vCenter through its provider configuration.

13
00:00:51.046 --> 00:00:56.093
A network mapping sends the source network to a Localnet network on OpenShift.

14
00:00:56.093 --> 00:01:00.965
A storage mapping sends the source datastore to OpenShift Data Foundation.

15
00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:03.562
Then the migration plan moves the machine.

16
00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:06.475
A warm migration copies the disk blocks in the background,

17
00:01:06.475 --> 00:01:08.784
while the source machine keeps running.

18
00:01:08.784 --> 00:01:11.313
At the cutover, the source machine shuts down,

19
00:01:11.313 --> 00:01:15.106
the last changes are copied, and the new machine starts on OpenShift.

20
00:01:16.306 --> 00:01:17.226
Once migrated,

21
00:01:17.226 --> 00:01:22.503
the machine joins a shared Localnet network called lab-common, on VLAN 500.

22
00:01:22.503 --> 00:01:30.098
This network reaches a dedicated subnet of the VPC for migrated machines,

23
00:01:30.098 --> 00:01:31.800
10.26.6.0/24.

24
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.422
Every lab has its own project, and all of them use this one shared network.

25
00:01:36.422 --> 00:01:41.144
Each machine needs a virtual network interface, or VNI, in that subnet.

26
00:01:41.144 --> 00:01:43.291
The lab team already made them for you.

27
00:01:44.491 --> 00:01:48.288
We start in the OpenShift web console, in our lab project.

28
00:01:48.288 --> 00:01:52.385
We open Migration for Virtualization, and click Migration plans.

29
00:01:52.385 --> 00:01:54.107
We click Create plan.

30
00:01:54.107 --> 00:01:57.354
We name the plan warm-migration-lab38.

31
00:01:57.354 --> 00:02:00.076
The plan project is our lab project.

32
00:02:00.076 --> 00:02:05.474
We choose the source provider ocp-gym-vc and the target provider host.

33
00:02:05.474 --> 00:02:09.296
We choose our lab project as the target project, and click Next.

34
00:02:14.524 --> 00:02:18.185
On the Virtual machines step, we type lab-38 in the filter,

35
00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:20.521
so only our own machine is left.

36
00:02:20.521 --> 00:02:25.143
We expand the lab-4399 folder, and tick lab-38.

37
00:02:25.143 --> 00:02:29.015
A warm migration needs the source machine to be on, and it is.

38
00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:29.962
We click Next.

39
00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:38.632
On the Network map step, we select lab38-networkmap, and click Next.

40
00:02:38.632 --> 00:02:43.104
On the Storage map step, we select lab38-storagemap, and click Next.

41
00:02:49.488 --> 00:02:53.160
On the Migration type step, we select Warm migration.

42
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.082
We click Skip to review.

43
00:02:55.082 --> 00:02:57.679
We check the summary, and click Create plan.

44
00:03:04.025 --> 00:03:05.847
The plan page opens.

45
00:03:05.847 --> 00:03:07.219
We click Start.

46
00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.441
The first copy begins, and the source machine stays on.

47
00:03:14.242 --> 00:03:16.764
We open the Virtual machines tab.

48
00:03:16.764 --> 00:03:17.828
During the pre-copy,

49
00:03:17.828 --> 00:03:21.636
the tool copies the disks while the source machine keeps working.

50
00:03:21.636 --> 00:03:25.633
This takes a few minutes, so we skip ahead to the end of the first copy.

51
00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:27.630
Now we look at the status.

52
00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.102
The Pipeline status changes from Running to Waiting.

53
00:03:31.102 --> 00:03:33.625
The migration now waits for the cutover.

54
00:03:34.825 --> 00:03:37.022
We click Schedule cutover.

55
00:03:37.022 --> 00:03:41.119
To cut over now, we click Set cutover, and keep the time as it is.

56
00:03:41.119 --> 00:03:44.666
The source machine shuts down, and the last changes are copied.

57
00:03:44.666 --> 00:03:47.538
The Pipeline status goes back to Running.

58
00:03:47.538 --> 00:03:50.110
We skip ahead to the end of the cutover.

59
00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.057
The status is now Succeeded.

60
00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:57.906
Last, we open Virtualization and VirtualMachines.

61
00:03:57.906 --> 00:04:02.578
Our machine, lab-38, now runs on OpenShift in our lab project.

62
00:04:02.578 --> 00:04:04.750
The only downtime was the cutover.

63
00:04:05.951 --> 00:04:10.573
You moved a VMware virtual machine to OpenShift with a warm migration.

64
00:04:10.573 --> 00:04:13.495
The source machine stayed online until the cutover.

